```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.2, dpi = 72,
                      fig.path = "figures/trends-", message = TRUE)
options(width = 100, tibble.width = 100, tibble.print_max = 12)
```

> **About this vignette.** All data are simulated with `wgh_simulate()`. The
> results show how the methods work and resemble the published patterns,
> but they are not the published findings and are not research results.
> The flows and functions run in development mode (`wgh_config("dev")`), with 100 bootstrap replicates where stated in the code.

This vignette reproduces the descriptive and trend analyses of two studies:

* secular trends in height, weight and BMI, nutritional status prevalence
  and regional differences (Ho et al. 2026) [1], and
* stunting, thinness, overweight, obesity and the double burden of
  malnutrition (Ho et al. 2026) [2].

It first builds each table and figure step by step with the module
functions, explaining the options and the output, and then shows the two
flows that produce everything in one call. The vignette is precomputed
because it uses a full-size simulated cohort.

## Data

`wgh_simulate()` builds a cohort that resembles the school health cohort of
the studies. With 30,000 children and the default seed it gives the same
teaching cohort as the books.

```{r}
library(WeGrowHealthR)
wgh_config("dev")            # fast settings, see ?wgh_config
sim <- wgh_simulate(30000, seed = 2026)
v <- sim$visits
dim(v)
head(v, 3)
```

`v` has one row per visit with WHO z-scores already computed. Each child is
measured at school once per year, so a child contributes several visits.

## 1. Describing the cohort (Table 1)

`wgh_table1()` summarises variables by group. Numeric variables are shown as
median (IQR) by default (`numeric_summary = "mean"` gives mean (SD)), and
categorical variables as n (%). `per_child = TRUE` uses each child's first
visit, which is what a cohort description needs, and a test of difference
between groups is added.

```{r}
cl <- wgh_classify(v)
cl$age_group <- wgh_age_bands(cl$age, breaks = c(0, 5, 11, 19),
                              labels = c("18 mo to 5 y", "5 to 10 y", "11 to 18 y"))
t1 <- wgh_table1(cl, vars = c("age", "haz", "baz", "site", "age_group"), by = "sex", per_child = TRUE)
t1
```

**Next step.** Export tables with `wgh_flextable()` (for Word) or
`wgh_export_xlsx()` (for Excel).

## 2. Height, weight and BMI by age and calendar year

The first study compared median measurements by single year of age across
calendar years. `wgh_median_ci()` gives medians with distribution-free
confidence intervals, and `wgh_trend_test(type = "mood_median")` tests
whether the medians differ across years within each sex and age.

```{r}
cl$age_year <- floor(cl$age)
meds <- wgh_median_ci(cl[cl$age >= 2, ], "height", by = c("sex", "age_year", "yearvisit"))
head(meds)
mt <- wgh_trend_test(cl[cl$age >= 2, ], "height", "yearvisit", by = c("sex", "age_year"), type = "mood_median")
head(mt)
```

```{r median-height, fig.alt = "Median height by age for each calendar year"}
mh <- aggregate(height ~ sex + age_year + yearvisit, data = cl[cl$age >= 2, ], FUN = median)
library(ggplot2)
ggplot(mh, aes(age_year, height, colour = factor(yearvisit))) +
  geom_line() + facet_wrap(~sex) + scale_colour_viridis_d(name = "Year") +
  theme_wgh() + labs(x = "Age (years)", y = "Median height (cm)")
```

The curves for different years lie almost on top of each other at young
ages and separate slightly in adolescence, the pattern of a slow secular
increase in height.

### First against last year, and site differences

`wgh_median_diff()` estimates the difference in medians between groups with
a bootstrap that resamples children, not visits, so repeated visits of the
same child are handled correctly. `ref` sets the reference group and `B`
the number of replicates (from the run mode when not given).

```{r}
yrs <- range(cl$yearvisit)
fl <- cl[cl$yearvisit %in% yrs & cl$age >= 2, ]
fl$year_f <- factor(fl$yearvisit)
md <- wgh_median_diff(fl, "height", "year_f", by = c("sex", "age_year"), B = 100)
head(md)
```

Each row is one sex and age, with the median difference (last minus first
year) and its bootstrap interval. The same function with `group = "site"`
gives the regional differences of the first study.

### Calendar trends with GEE

Prevalence and medians by year ignore that the same children are measured
again. `wgh_gee_trend()` fits generalised estimating equations with an
exchangeable working correlation within children, giving the change per
calendar year within age groups. For a continuous outcome it is a linear
GEE (cm per year), and for a TRUE/FALSE indicator a logistic GEE (odds ratio
per year).

```{r}
gee_h <- wgh_gee_trend(cl[cl$age >= 5, ], "height", age_group = "age_group", by = "sex")
gee_h$slopes
```

`estimate` is the change in mean height per calendar year at the same age,
in cm, with its 95% interval. Values of a few millimetres per year describe
a slow secular increase in height.

## 3. Nutritional status prevalence

`wgh_prevalence()` with a status variable gives the share of each category.
`method = "sison_glaz"` gives simultaneous 95% intervals for all categories,
so the intervals can be compared with each other. Prevalence from 5 years
and below 5 years are reported separately, because the WHO cut-offs differ.

```{r}
older <- cl[cl$age >= 5, ]
wgh_prevalence(older, "bmi_status", by = "sex", method = "sison_glaz")
```

Overweight and obesity by year and by site, with Cochran-Armitage trend
tests:

```{r}
pv_year <- wgh_prevalence(older, "overweight_obesity", by = c("sex", "yearvisit"))
pv_site <- wgh_prevalence(older, "overweight_obesity", by = c("sex", "site"))
pv_site
wgh_trend_test(older, "overweight_obesity", "yearvisit", by = "sex")
```

```{r prevalence-trend, fig.alt = "Overweight or obesity by year and sex"}
wgh_plot_prevalence(pv_year, title = "Overweight or obesity, 5 to 18 years")
```

```{r status-bars, fig.alt = "Nutritional status by age group and sex", fig.height = 4.6}
wgh_plot_status_bars(cl, group = "age_group")
```

Boys have a higher prevalence of overweight and obesity than girls, one of
the main findings of the first study, and the simulated cohort reproduces it.

## 4. Stunting, thinness, overweight and obesity

The second study looked at all forms of malnutrition together. The same
functions are applied to each indicator. `wgh_gee_trend()` gives the odds
ratio per calendar year within age groups. Age groups with fewer than
`min_events` cases are skipped with a warning, because a logistic GEE with
very few events does not converge.

```{r}
cl$age_group4 <- wgh_age_bands(cl$age, breaks = c(0, 5, 10, 15, 19))
ind <- c("stunting", "thinness", "overweight", "obesity")
pv_all <- do.call(rbind, lapply(ind, function(o)
  cbind(indicator = o, wgh_prevalence(cl, o, by = c("sex", "yearvisit")))))
head(pv_all)
gee_ob <- wgh_gee_trend(cl, "obesity", age_group = "age_group4", by = "sex")
gee_ob$slopes
```

An odds ratio below 1 means the odds of obesity fall by that factor each
year within the age group.

```{r forest, fig.alt = "Odds ratio of obesity per calendar year by age group", fig.height = 3.6}
g <- gee_ob$slopes
g$row <- paste(g$sex, g$age_group, sep = ", ")
wgh_plot_forest(g, "row", "or_per_year", "or_lower", "or_upper", colour = "sex", ref = 1, log = TRUE,
                x_label = "Odds ratio per calendar year")
```

## 5. The double burden of malnutrition

The double burden can be measured in a child (stunting together with
overweight) or in a population (stunting and overweight both common in the
same group). `wgh_dbm()` returns both.

```{r}
dbm <- wgh_dbm(cl, by = c("sex", "site", "yearvisit"))
head(dbm$population)
```

`dbm_individual` is the share of children who are both stunted and
overweight, and `dbm_population` flags groups where stunting is at least
10% and overweight at least 20%, the usual population definition.

```{r dbm-heatmap, fig.alt = "Individual double burden by site and year", fig.height = 3.6}
dd <- dbm$population
dd$label <- paste(dd$sex, dd$site)
wgh_plot_heatmap(dd, "yearvisit", "label", "dbm_individual", digits = 2)
```

## 6. Both studies in one call

The flows run all of the above with the settings of the studies and return
a `wgh_report`.

```{r}
trends <- wgh_flow_secular_trends(v, fit_lms = FALSE)
trends
malnutrition <- wgh_flow_malnutrition_trends(v)
malnutrition
```

The printed report lists its tables and figures and the status of each
model. A model that failed or was skipped is shown with the reason, so
nothing is silently missing.

```{r flow-figure, fig.alt = "Prevalence of the four indicators by year", fig.height = 4.6}
plot(malnutrition, "fig_trends", title = "Malnutrition indicators by year")
```

```{r, eval = FALSE}
malnutrition$tables$gee_odds_ratios   # any table by name
wgh_export(malnutrition, "malnutrition_results")
```

With `fit_lms = TRUE` (default) the trends flow also fits cohort LMS height
centiles with `gamlss` (see `wgh_fit_lms()` and `plot.wgh_lms()`).

## Where to go next

* `vignette("trajectories")` follows children over time instead of
  comparing cross-sections.
* `vignette("growth-distributions")` asks whether BMI is related to height
  differently in short and tall children.

